    "record": "2026-10-22T20-24-25Z-why-does-updating-candidate-skills.json",
    "label": "Q6",
    "section": "realistic",
    "mark": "right",
    "note": "The answer explains how the diff works and gives two of the reasons I expected: it keeps the stored capitalisation and respects the unique index on skill names. It misses that a diff also avoids needless database writes. The deeper reason is in JobHunt's rationale file, which the tool does not read yet: the skills table can only insert and delete, never update, by design.",
    "source_path": "experiments/0018-realistic-usage-questions/README.md",
    "source_row": "Q6"
  },
  {
    "record": "2026-10-22T20-24-31Z-why-does-hitting-a-usage.json",
    "label": "Q7",
    "section": "realistic",
    "mark": "right",
    "note": "Right, because the answer gives the core reason: hitting a usage limit is a refusal, a normal outcome, not a failure reported as an error. It misses why JobHunt cares: counting refusals as failures would set off the error alert while everything works as intended. That reason is in spec 0011's rationale file, which the tool does not read yet. Partly right would also be a fair mark.",
    "source_path": "experiments/0018-realistic-usage-questions/README.md",
    "source_row": "Q7"
